WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.568 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is a "New York
Times" best-selling author,

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.772
Politico's White House bureau
chief and the host of

00:00:05.772 --> 00:00:09.409
"Way Too Early," which airs
weekdays at 5:00 a.m. on MSNBC.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:12.078
Please welcome to the show
Jonathan Lemire, everybody.

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.513
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:20.019 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:20.019 --> 00:00:26.492 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:26.492 --> 00:00:28.294
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:00:28.294 --> 00:00:29.562
I know at this point,
it's already been

00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.564
a very long day for you.
-Yeah, it's past my bedtime.

00:00:31.564 --> 00:00:33.499
-It's past your bedtime.
What time --

00:00:33.499 --> 00:00:35.735
First of all, I really like that
the title of your show

00:00:35.735 --> 00:00:38.638
"Way Too Early" is obvious --
it's honest I should say

00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:40.306
about exactly when it's on TV.

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.375
-Yeah, it is truth
in advertising.

00:00:42.375 --> 00:00:44.177
It is 5:00 a.m. Eastern.

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:47.279
It is a great thing to be able
to start the day,

00:00:47.279 --> 00:00:49.782
presenting the news of the day,
what people need to know

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:51.150
as they're getting ready
for their day,

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:53.319
but, Seth, it is --
it is way too early.

00:00:53.319 --> 00:00:56.222
-Yeah, so what time do you
actually have to

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.590
set an alarm in the morning?

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.194
-So, my schedule in the morning
is the alarm goes off

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:03.329
at 3:10 a.m.
[ Audience groans ]

00:01:03.329 --> 00:01:06.599
And at 3:11 a.m., I question
all of my life's decisions.

00:01:06.599 --> 00:01:08.400
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:11.370
-That's followed by a few sort
of incoherent snoozes

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:14.106
and some coffee, and try not to
spill it on myself

00:01:14.106 --> 00:01:15.875
as I get dressed in the dark.

00:01:15.875 --> 00:01:20.046
I will say, though, coming into
the studio here at Rock Center,

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.915
or if I'm in D.C.,
no traffic at that hour.

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:24.350
-Really?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.718
-So that's sort of a --
-You sail right in.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:27.253
-New York City life hack.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:29.822
-It is very nice because I think
the most helpful time

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:31.557
for a lot of people to get
their news would be

00:01:31.557 --> 00:01:32.891
first thing of the day, right?

00:01:32.891 --> 00:01:34.427
These are the things they need
to be aware of today.

00:01:34.427 --> 00:01:37.297
Have you had people come up
to you with an appreciation?

00:01:37.297 --> 00:01:39.132
Obviously, there's a certain
kind of person who's watching

00:01:39.132 --> 00:01:40.500
the news at 5:00 in the morning.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.802
They must appreciate that
there's someone like you

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.405
who's, you know, putting
their effort into it.

00:01:45.405 --> 00:01:47.873
-Insomniacs. That's the word
you're looking for.

00:01:47.873 --> 00:01:50.376
We do get some night owls from
the West Coast who haven't gone

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:53.178
to bed yet but we do; there are
people who are up early

00:01:53.178 --> 00:01:55.081
because their days start early,
and that could be

00:01:55.081 --> 00:01:59.185
a corporate CEO or someone,
you know, driving a city bus.

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:02.321
And we try to deliver that news
to everyone.

00:02:02.321 --> 00:02:03.455
It is a start to the day.

00:02:03.455 --> 00:02:04.691
And we know mornings
are about routines,

00:02:04.691 --> 00:02:07.059
you're trying to get out
the door, and my show,

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:11.130
and then I'm around on "Morning
Joe" afterwards for a few hours.

00:02:11.130 --> 00:02:13.700
It is -- It's a responsibility
that we take seriously.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:15.168
-And what time do you go to bed?

00:02:15.168 --> 00:02:20.072
-So the goal is around
9:00 p.m.,

00:02:20.072 --> 00:02:21.641
which is still not
nearly enough sleep,

00:02:21.641 --> 00:02:24.911
and I know that, but we --
you know, two kids at home,

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:27.279
two boys, 11 and 8,
so that's pushing it,

00:02:27.279 --> 00:02:29.215
because their bedtime
is supposed to be 8:30,

00:02:29.215 --> 00:02:30.583
quarter to 9:00.

00:02:30.583 --> 00:02:33.452
Sometimes, they get a kick out
of tucking Dad into bed

00:02:33.452 --> 00:02:36.122
and then they get to play
another hour of "Mario Kart."

00:02:36.122 --> 00:02:37.689
-You also --

00:02:37.689 --> 00:02:39.192
You've obviously been
a traveling reporter as well.

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.427
You covered the 2016
presidential campaign.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:44.096
You received a lot of attention
for a question

00:02:44.096 --> 00:02:46.332
you asked
Donald Trump in Helsinki,

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.668
when he was on stage,
very famously,

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:50.469
with Russian President
Vladimir Putin.

00:02:50.469 --> 00:02:52.438
Can you tell us a little bit
about the question

00:02:52.438 --> 00:02:53.539
and what it was like
in the moment

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:55.074
as you were asking
those two gentlemen?

00:02:55.074 --> 00:02:57.043
-Sure, so this is
the summer of 2018.

00:02:57.043 --> 00:03:00.213
This was then-president Trump's
much anticipated first

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.448
and only summit
with Vladimir Putin.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.717
There, of course, had been
questions for years

00:03:04.717 --> 00:03:06.719
about what connections
he may have to Russia.

00:03:06.719 --> 00:03:09.288
This was the height
of the Mueller probe,

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:12.591
the investigation into his ties
there, potential ties to Moscow,

00:03:12.591 --> 00:03:14.627
and I was at the Associated
Press at the time.

00:03:14.627 --> 00:03:17.496
And it was, you know,
it's a limited press conference.

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:18.864
Only a couple reporters
got questions.

00:03:18.864 --> 00:03:20.533
And I was called on.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:21.867
And I asked --
-Did you know?

00:03:21.867 --> 00:03:24.103
Is that something where you know
or do you have a sense?

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:25.337
-No, no.
-Okay.

00:03:25.337 --> 00:03:28.074
-You don't.
I mean, it's --

00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:30.310
I certainly had been
petitioning to say

00:03:30.310 --> 00:03:31.710
that I'd like to get a question.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.946
This is a big moment, important
things need to be asked

00:03:33.946 --> 00:03:36.849
but you don't know until that --
until that moment.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.418
Often, Donald Trump would try --

00:03:39.418 --> 00:03:40.887
choose not to answer
my questions.

00:03:40.887 --> 00:03:42.754
He would go out of his way
to ignore.

00:03:42.754 --> 00:03:44.523
-Usually, he's such a straight
shooter. That's weird.

00:03:44.523 --> 00:03:45.458
-Yeah, it was surprising.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:46.659
-He was probably jet-lagged.

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:47.760
-Yeah, maybe
that's what it was.

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.362
But he did this time and the way
it works

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:52.765
in these
presidential news conferences,

00:03:52.765 --> 00:03:55.001
you get one question
to each leader.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:57.303
So to President Trump,
I simply asked him,

00:03:57.303 --> 00:04:00.607
I said, you know, the U.S.

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:02.475
Intelligence officials,
all of them,

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.610
say that Moscow,
Russia interfered

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:06.679
with the 2016 election.
Vladimir Putin,

00:04:06.679 --> 00:04:08.714
the man standing next to you
just denied it.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:10.216
Who do you believe?

00:04:10.216 --> 00:04:12.418
And he pretty clearly signaled
that he believed Russia,

00:04:12.418 --> 00:04:13.652
that he did not believe
the assessment

00:04:13.652 --> 00:04:15.321
of his own intelligence
agencies.

00:04:15.321 --> 00:04:18.491
And then my question
to Vladimir Putin was,

00:04:18.491 --> 00:04:21.894
did the Russians have
any compromising material

00:04:21.894 --> 00:04:23.662
on Donald Trump or his family?

00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:25.798
You may recall there was some
salacious rumors back then

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:28.234
as to stuff they may or may not
have had, some kompromat,

00:04:28.234 --> 00:04:30.703
and I will note as Putin
answered the question,

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:31.737
and by the way,
when he talks to you,

00:04:31.737 --> 00:04:33.872
he doesn't break eye contact.

00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:36.475
It's pretty terrifying
and I made sure not to have --

00:04:36.475 --> 00:04:38.811
I made sure not to have any tea
or step near a window

00:04:38.811 --> 00:04:43.015
or a set of stairs
the rest of the day.

00:04:43.015 --> 00:04:46.018
He gave a long answer about
how it would be very challenging

00:04:46.018 --> 00:04:48.654
to collect compromising material
on every prominent

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.790
American who comes to Russia.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:52.958
He never actually denied it.

00:04:52.958 --> 00:04:54.560
And I will say a postscript,
obviously,

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.895
that moment,
the Helsinki news conference,

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:58.831
was seen as one very damaging
for Donald Trump.

00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:00.700
Even some of his allies
in the Republican Party

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.602
turned on him and said
they couldn't believe --

00:05:02.602 --> 00:05:04.837
I think John McCain
used the word "traitorous."

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.072
That we got back on Air Force
One -- I was part

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:08.808
of the presidential pool,
so I was traveling with him

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:12.311
back to Washington,
and he got on the plane

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:13.479
and had thought
he had done well.

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.214
And as soon as he turned
on Fox News,

00:05:15.214 --> 00:05:17.249
he was disabused of that notion

00:05:17.249 --> 00:05:21.320
and grew so angry
that aides later told me

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:23.155
that he was so anxious
to get home,

00:05:23.155 --> 00:05:25.324
you know how planes sometimes,
the pilot will tell you,

00:05:25.324 --> 00:05:26.959
"Well, we can make up
some time in the air."

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:29.228
Air Force One really has
the ability to do that.

00:05:29.228 --> 00:05:32.064
[ Laughter ]
And we shaved about two hours

00:05:32.064 --> 00:05:33.466
off our flight
time from Finland.

00:05:33.466 --> 00:05:35.501
The plane was like shaking
on the way back,

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:36.702
and we're all hanging on

00:05:36.702 --> 00:05:38.370
as he was looking
to put this behind him.

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:39.739
-Now, I want to ask.

00:05:39.739 --> 00:05:42.408
Something obviously you've been
reporting on last week

00:05:42.408 --> 00:05:46.212
and into this week is the
classified documents situation.

00:05:46.212 --> 00:05:48.047
Obviously,
we were all well aware

00:05:48.047 --> 00:05:49.415
of what had happened
with the documents

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.550
that former president Trump
was keeping at Mar-A-Lago.

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.320
And now, we're having obviously
a far smaller amount

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:56.022
of documents,
but real classified documents

00:05:56.022 --> 00:05:59.225
that were in President Biden's
possession.

00:05:59.225 --> 00:06:01.928
What -- How do you see
this story unfolding?

00:06:01.928 --> 00:06:05.397
Do you see this as a sort of
universal idea

00:06:05.397 --> 00:06:07.800
that keeping classified material
is bad

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.569
or do you see it developing
into sort of a partisan story

00:06:10.569 --> 00:06:11.804
where we're losing our focus?

00:06:11.804 --> 00:06:13.306
-Yeah, we're certainly
covering this every day,

00:06:13.306 --> 00:06:14.940
both on MSNBC and Politico.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:17.910
And it is much more
of a political story

00:06:17.910 --> 00:06:20.779
than it is a legal one
for President Biden.

00:06:20.779 --> 00:06:22.581
There's certainly, at least
from what we know of now,

00:06:22.581 --> 00:06:24.750
and it's still developing,
we're still learning about it.

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:26.986
It seems that there's about
a dozen or so documents

00:06:26.986 --> 00:06:28.187
seemingly inadvertently

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.955
packed away
where they shouldn't have been.

00:06:29.955 --> 00:06:32.391
And to be clear, the classified
material should not be

00:06:32.391 --> 00:06:33.592
in the garage of a home

00:06:33.592 --> 00:06:35.995
in Wilmington, Delaware,
that's never okay.

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:38.797
But there's no sense of intent
that it was done deliberately,

00:06:38.797 --> 00:06:41.133
and President Biden,
unlike his predecessor,

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:43.869
has not been accused of trying
to willfully obstruct efforts

00:06:43.869 --> 00:06:45.437
to get that material back.

00:06:45.437 --> 00:06:49.442
So we have now descended
into very much a partisan story

00:06:49.442 --> 00:06:51.643
though national security
implications to be clear,

00:06:51.643 --> 00:06:52.779
classified materials,

00:06:52.779 --> 00:06:54.147
that matters,
that's serious stuff,

00:06:54.147 --> 00:06:58.217
but this is much more
about politics

00:06:58.217 --> 00:06:59.918
and we're seeing
from a lot of Republicans,

00:06:59.918 --> 00:07:01.987
I mean, hypocrisy,
there's no other way to say it.

00:07:01.987 --> 00:07:04.656
They didn't care much at all
about the hundreds

00:07:04.656 --> 00:07:06.592
upon hundreds of documents
found at Mar-A-Lago.

00:07:06.592 --> 00:07:08.260
They seem to care quite a bit
about this dozen

00:07:08.260 --> 00:07:10.295
or so found
in Delaware and D.C.

00:07:10.295 --> 00:07:12.098
-Another hypocrisy you've
pointed out

00:07:12.098 --> 00:07:14.166
is they are sort of raising
a ruckus

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:16.001
about the fact that there were
no visitor logs

00:07:16.001 --> 00:07:18.904
for who came to President
Biden's home

00:07:18.904 --> 00:07:21.039
in Wilmington, Delaware.

00:07:21.039 --> 00:07:23.208
But this is obviously,
if you were a supporter

00:07:23.208 --> 00:07:24.443
of President Trump,

00:07:24.443 --> 00:07:26.178
this would be the sort of
height of hypocrisy

00:07:26.178 --> 00:07:28.347
because visitor logs
weren't really his thing.

00:07:28.347 --> 00:07:29.481
-They were not.

00:07:29.481 --> 00:07:30.982
I mean, first of all,
for any president,

00:07:30.982 --> 00:07:33.886
it's a personal residence.
Visitor logs are not kept,

00:07:33.886 --> 00:07:35.153
whether it's President
Biden's home,

00:07:35.153 --> 00:07:36.422
Wilmington, Delaware,

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:38.157
President Obama's
home in Chicago,

00:07:38.157 --> 00:07:41.294
President Bush's home in Texas
or any of President

00:07:41.294 --> 00:07:42.495
Trump's homes,
Mar-A-Lago,

00:07:42.495 --> 00:07:44.096
Bedminster,
Trump Tower, et cetera.

00:07:44.096 --> 00:07:46.465
But more than that,
President Trump did away

00:07:46.465 --> 00:07:48.634
with the tradition
of releasing visitor

00:07:48.634 --> 00:07:51.169
logs at the White House itself.

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:52.839
So it is very much one

00:07:52.839 --> 00:07:55.941
where Republicans don't really
have a credible leg to stand on.

00:07:55.941 --> 00:07:57.176
-He's not watching right now,

00:07:57.176 --> 00:07:59.345
but if President
Trump was watching --

00:07:59.345 --> 00:08:00.913
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.913 --> 00:08:03.315
I think he would like what you
just said because he'd be like,

00:08:03.315 --> 00:08:05.117
"Did you hear
I had a lot of houses?"

00:08:05.117 --> 00:08:06.953
[ Laughter ]

00:08:06.953 --> 00:08:09.121
"Do you hear how he mentioned
only one Obama?"

00:08:09.121 --> 00:08:12.658
[ Laughter and applause ]
-That's true.

00:08:12.658 --> 00:08:13.893
-"He had to mention
like a bunch of mine.

00:08:13.893 --> 00:08:14.994
He missed a few.

00:08:14.994 --> 00:08:17.563
[ Laughter ]

00:08:17.563 --> 00:08:20.766
There's a story,
obviously it's being covered.

00:08:20.766 --> 00:08:23.669
I think that people maybe
haven't quite wrapped

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:26.939
their heads around how insane
this George Santos story is.

00:08:26.939 --> 00:08:28.574
This is a freshman
Republican Congressman

00:08:28.574 --> 00:08:31.243
from New York
who was elected,

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:35.915
and then his personal biography
just seems every day

00:08:35.915 --> 00:08:37.917
is falling apart even more.

00:08:37.917 --> 00:08:40.452
Maybe hadn't told a single truth
about himself.

00:08:40.452 --> 00:08:42.822
Have you ever seen
anything quite like this?

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.723
-Do you have a favorite
George Santos lie?

00:08:44.723 --> 00:08:48.627
-I -- I mean, Jew --
saying you're Jewish

00:08:48.627 --> 00:08:51.263
and then backing off
and clarifying

00:08:51.263 --> 00:08:56.536
that you meant you were
the "ish."

00:08:56.536 --> 00:08:58.170
Personally, I found that one
very --

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:00.105
-That's classic.

00:09:00.105 --> 00:09:02.140
Another 10 out of 10
no-notes lie

00:09:02.140 --> 00:09:04.042
is that he claimed to be
a volleyball star

00:09:04.042 --> 00:09:06.812
at Baruch College
that won a championship.

00:09:06.812 --> 00:09:08.380
That part's true. Baruch College
won a championship.

00:09:08.380 --> 00:09:10.349
He had nothing to do with it.
He wasn't on the team.

00:09:10.349 --> 00:09:11.517
He didn't go to the school.

00:09:11.517 --> 00:09:13.819
But more than that
that he played so hard,

00:09:13.819 --> 00:09:16.188
he had to have
double knee replacement.

00:09:16.188 --> 00:09:18.924
He had to have both knees
replaced, a man in his 30s.

00:09:18.924 --> 00:09:20.726
He seems pretty spry as he's
trying to evade

00:09:20.726 --> 00:09:23.629
reporters in Capitol Hill
hallways.

00:09:23.629 --> 00:09:25.731
But to your point,
it is remarkable.

00:09:25.731 --> 00:09:28.000
Certainly we live --

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:31.770
We've never had a politician
lie A to Z about his biography.

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.972
George Santos has done that,
if that is his real name.

00:09:33.972 --> 00:09:37.142
[ Laughter ]
And what we -- But in some ways,

00:09:37.142 --> 00:09:41.080
it almost seems like the natural
evolution of the Trump era.

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:44.917
A figure who so much of
his career was built on lies.

00:09:44.917 --> 00:09:48.453
And then of course from his
campaign to his administration,

00:09:48.453 --> 00:09:50.355
he would so distrust
with institutions

00:09:50.355 --> 00:09:52.190
by telling lie
after lie after lie,

00:09:52.190 --> 00:09:54.327
and of course, most of all
about the 2020 election,

00:09:54.327 --> 00:09:56.662
which is what I covered
in my book.

00:09:56.662 --> 00:09:59.765
In some ways, Santos is like,
"Well, he got inspiration."

00:09:59.765 --> 00:10:02.534
-[ Laughs ]
Absolutely.

00:10:02.534 --> 00:10:03.603
How do you see it ending?

00:10:03.603 --> 00:10:05.003
Do you think he's going
to stick it out?

00:10:05.003 --> 00:10:07.506
-Well, the biggest surprise
to me in terms of the day-to-day

00:10:07.506 --> 00:10:09.207
is the fact there have been
some of his fellow

00:10:09.207 --> 00:10:11.777
Republicans who have called
for him to resign,

00:10:11.777 --> 00:10:15.314
including those in New York,
as well as Nassau County --

00:10:15.314 --> 00:10:16.748
that's where he represents,
Long Island --

00:10:16.748 --> 00:10:19.217
that Republican Party
have said, "You should go."

00:10:19.217 --> 00:10:20.986
He is saying he won't.

00:10:20.986 --> 00:10:22.488
But an element --
at a time of such great

00:10:22.488 --> 00:10:23.922
partisanship,
the fact that any Republicans

00:10:23.922 --> 00:10:26.025
are willing to say
he should go is noteworthy.

00:10:26.025 --> 00:10:27.927
That said, Republican leadership

00:10:27.927 --> 00:10:29.929
in the House,
they're not doing so.

00:10:29.929 --> 00:10:31.497
They're even giving him
a committee assignment.

00:10:31.497 --> 00:10:33.699
Kevin McCarthy can't afford
a single vote.

00:10:33.699 --> 00:10:36.935
And the other lesson that Santos
seemed to learn from Trump --

00:10:36.935 --> 00:10:38.203
you never apologize,

00:10:38.203 --> 00:10:39.538
you never say you're sorry,
you don't quit.

00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:40.939
He thinks he can ride it out.

00:10:40.939 --> 00:10:42.774
-Yeah, he learned all of that
from his volleyball coach.

00:10:42.774 --> 00:10:45.043
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:10:45.043 --> 00:10:46.545
-One last thing to ask you
about, which is sports,

00:10:46.545 --> 00:10:49.982
we are Boston sports fans.
You're a Boston Red Sox fan.

00:10:49.982 --> 00:10:53.185
You are raising children
in New York City.

00:10:53.185 --> 00:10:54.887
This is a great fear of mine.

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:56.922
Ours are a little younger.
They haven't committed.

00:10:56.922 --> 00:11:00.326
But do you ever worry they're
going to come home one day

00:11:00.326 --> 00:11:03.629
with some Yankee paraphernalia
or tell you this is the team

00:11:03.629 --> 00:11:05.964
that they have chosen
to throw their life behind?

00:11:05.964 --> 00:11:07.466
-I mean, they know the stakes.
They'd be disowned.

00:11:07.466 --> 00:11:09.901
-Yeah, okay.
-So no, there's no chance.

00:11:09.901 --> 00:11:11.837
They are being raised
as Red Sox fans,

00:11:11.837 --> 00:11:13.672
I've lived in New York
a long time.

00:11:13.672 --> 00:11:15.807
It has done nothing to diminish
my fandom

00:11:15.807 --> 00:11:18.110
for the Red Sox.
In fact, living among

00:11:18.110 --> 00:11:21.547
Yankee fans every day just makes
me dislike them so much more.

00:11:21.547 --> 00:11:23.081
-Yeah, I agree.
[ Laughter ]

00:11:23.081 --> 00:11:26.485
And it was all worth it
to live here in 2004.

00:11:26.485 --> 00:11:28.454
-Was it ever, oh, was it ever.

00:11:28.454 --> 00:11:30.655
-That was the time of my life.

00:11:30.655 --> 00:11:34.493
-And my kids know, as wonderful
as it was when they were born,

00:11:34.493 --> 00:11:36.795
that's second place to 2004.

00:11:36.795 --> 00:11:38.463
-Everything's been downhill
since then.

00:11:38.463 --> 00:11:40.332
You guys,
that's Jonathan Lemire.

00:11:40.332 --> 00:11:41.666
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:11:41.666 --> 00:11:44.770
"Way Too Early" airs weekdays
at 5:00 a.m. on MSNBC.

